{"answer":"VERDICT: found_break\n\nCONSENSUS_BLOCKING: yes\n\nRATIONALE:\nMercury’s alleged alias-matcher defect is contradicted by the actual caller chain. Two different defects are supported by selected-source inspection:\n\n1. **Executable trust no longer prevents dispatch.** `trai_brain/mercury-bridge/codex-challenger.js:89` computes `trusted: false` for a package/version mismatch, but line 96 still marks initialization complete and line 119 sends the review prompt to that executable. The former client checked ownership, writable paths and executable provenance before dispatch (`trai_brain/mercury-bridge/llm-client.js:96`, `:118`, `:498`). The existing hard-boundary handler consumes errors carrying failed trust metadata (`trai_brain/mercury-bridge/adversarial-review.js:30`); the new client merely returns that metadata. This weakens the expressly preserved executable boundary. No compromised installation is alleged.\n\n2. **Transport failure can be reported as provider readiness.** The new client returns `termination: 'error'` after an invocation error (`trai_brain/mercury-bridge/codex-challenger.js:122`, `:145`). `trai_brain/mercury-bridge/provider-preflight.js:191` nevertheless sets `ok: true`, while line 201 correctly marks the attempt failed. Lines 248–250 convert that contradictory result into challenger readiness and overall success when Mercury succeeds. The CLI consumes that success at `trai_brain/mercury-bridge/ask.js:1845`; the ledger labels it `provider_preflight_pass` at `trai_brain/mercury-bridge/run-ledger.js:1172`. This is a producer/consumer mismatch, not a reason to suppress answers or add response-validation throws.\n\nDISAGREEMENT:\nMercury assumes Astra passes `\"astra\"` into `claudeAppliedModelMatchesAlias`. The configured model is `gpt-6-astra`, and the factory constructs `CodexChallengerClient` (`trai_brain/mercury-bridge/llm-client.js:735`, `:842`). Its metadata supplies its own identity posture (`trai_brain/mercury-bridge/codex-challenge